canonical_claim.statement:
As printed (p. 348): ‘Here, we state a weaker claim verifiable at full generality, which has been known since Copas [(1973), equation (9)]: for completely randomized experiments, if outcomes are binary, then under the sharp null hypothesis of no treatment effect, all test statistics may be written as a function of the difference-in-means statistic. Stronger statements are likely available.’ As printed (p. 346, item (iii)): ‘We also note an error in Ding’s (2017) Theorem 7 and suggest a refinement that is correct at full generality.’

Corrected wording (Sol's smallest repair, validated.json; doubt artifact, section 8). Section 4: ‘For completely randomized experiments, if outcomes are binary, then under the sharp null hypothesis of no treatment effect, all test statistics based on the two-by-two treatment-by-outcome table may be written as a function of the difference-in-means statistic.’ Introduction, item (iii): append ‘for statistics based on the two-by-two treatment-by-outcome table’ to ‘a refinement that is correct at full generality’.

Formal content, with every hypothesis. Let $N_1\ge1$ and $N_0\ge1$ be integers and $N=N_1+N_0$. Let $y=(y_1,\ldots,y_N)\in\{0,1\}^N$ be fixed: the outcomes are binary and, under the sharp null $Y_i(1)=Y_i(0)=y_i$ for every $i$, the observed outcome of unit $i$ is $y_i$ under every assignment. Let $M=\sum_iy_i$. Let $\Omega=\{z\in\{0,1\}^N:\sum_iz_i=N_1\}$ be the set of assignments of a completely randomized experiment (they are equiprobable, but no probability enters the statement). For $z\in\Omega$ let $a(z)=\sum_iz_iy_i$ be the number of treated successes, let $C(z)=\big(a(z),\ N_1-a(z),\ M-a(z),\ N_0-M+a(z)\big)$ be the two-by-two table (treated successes, treated failures, control successes, control failures), and let $\widehat{\tau}(z)=\frac{a(z)}{N_1}-\frac{M-a(z)}{N_0}$ be the difference in means. Then:

(a) for every $z\in\Omega$: $\widehat{\tau}(z)=\frac{N\,a(z)-N_1M}{N_1N_0}$ and $a(z)=\frac{N_1N_0\,\widehat{\tau}(z)+N_1M}{N_1+N_0}$;

(b) for all $z,z'\in\Omega$: $\widehat{\tau}(z)=\widehat{\tau}(z')$ if and only if $a(z)=a(z')$, if and only if $C(z)=C(z')$;

(c) [the corrected claim] for every set $\mathcal{S}$ and every function $h:\mathbb{Z}^4\to\mathcal{S}$ there is a function $g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathcal{S}$ such that $h(C(z))=g(\widehat{\tau}(z))$ for every $z\in\Omega$. The function $g$ does not depend on $z$; it can be chosen to depend on $y$ only through $M$, namely $g(t)=h\big(A(t),\ N_1-A(t),\ M-A(t),\ N_0-M+A(t)\big)$ with $A(t)=\frac{N_1N_0\,t+N_1M}{N}$ at the values $t$ that $\widehat{\tau}$ takes on $\Omega$, and arbitrary elsewhere. No monotonicity of $h$ is assumed, so the statement covers statistics such as $|\widehat{\tau}|$;

(d) [statistics of the two outcome samples are table statistics] let $F:\{0,1\}^{N_1}\times\{0,1\}^{N_0}\to\mathcal{S}$ satisfy $F(x_1\circ\pi_1,\ x_0\circ\pi_0)=F(x_1,x_0)$ for all permutations $\pi_1$ of $\{1,\ldots,N_1\}$ and $\pi_0$ of $\{1,\ldots,N_0\}$. For $z\in\Omega$ let $x_1(z)\in\{0,1\}^{N_1}$ list the outcomes $y_i$ of the treated units ($z_i=1$) in increasing order of $i$, and $x_0(z)\in\{0,1\}^{N_0}$ those of the control units. Then $F(x_1(z),x_0(z))=h_F(C(z))$ for every $z\in\Omega$, where $h_F(a,\cdot,b,\cdot)=F\big(c_{N_1}(a),c_{N_0}(b)\big)$ and $c_n(k)=(1,\ldots,1,0,\ldots,0)$ has $k$ ones followed by $n-k$ zeros; hence, by (c), $F(x_1(z),x_0(z))=g(\widehat{\tau}(z))$ on $\Omega$ for some $g$. Conversely, for every $h$ the map $(x_1,x_0)\mapsto h\big(|x_1|,\ N_1-|x_1|,\ |x_0|,\ N_0-|x_0|\big)$, where $|x|$ is the number of ones, has this invariance;

(e) [exact criterion] for an arbitrary function $T:\Omega\to\mathcal{S}$: there is $g$ with $T(z)=g(\widehat{\tau}(z))$ for every $z\in\Omega$ if and only if $T(z)=T(z')$ whenever $C(z)=C(z')$.

Printed statement, refuted in its unrestricted literal reading (p. 348): ‘Here, we state a weaker claim verifiable at full generality, which has been known since Copas [(1973), equation (9)]: for completely randomized experiments, if outcomes are binary, then under the sharp null hypothesis of no treatment effect, all test statistics may be written as a function of the difference-in-means statistic.’

Reading refuted. ‘Test statistic’: any real-valued function $T$ of the assignment vector on the randomization support, for a fixed population that satisfies the sharp null; it may use unit labels or fixed pretreatment covariates. ‘May be written as a function of the difference-in-means statistic’: there is one function $g$ (it may depend on the fixed population, the covariates and the arm sizes, not on the realized assignment) with $T(z)=g(\widehat{\tau}(z))$ for every assignment $z$ in the support.

Counterexample (validators, exact arithmetic). $N=4$, $N_1=N_0=2$. Potential outcomes $Y(1)=Y(0)=y=(1,1,0,0)$: binary, and the sharp null holds. Complete randomization: $\Omega$ is the set of the six $z\in\{0,1\}^4$ with $\sum_iz_i=2$, each with probability $1/6$. $\widehat{\tau}(z)=\frac12\sum_iz_iy_i-\frac12\sum_i(1-z_i)y_i$. Let $z_A=(1,0,1,0)$ and $z_B=(0,1,1,0)$. Then:

(i) $z_A,z_B\in\Omega$, $\widehat{\tau}(z_A)=\widehat{\tau}(z_B)=0$, and the two assignments have the same two-by-two treatment-by-outcome table, with all four cells equal to $1$;

(ii) [no covariate] for $L(z)=z_1y_1$: $L(z_A)=1$ and $L(z_B)=0$;

(iii) [a fixed binary pretreatment covariate $x=(1,0,1,0)$; the difference in means of $x_iy_i$] for $T(z)=\frac12\sum_iz_ix_iy_i-\frac12\sum_i(1-z_i)x_iy_i$: $T(z_A)=1/2$ and $T(z_B)=-1/2$;

(iv) hence there is no function $g$ with $L(z)=g(\widehat{\tau}(z))$ for every $z\in\Omega$, and none with $T(z)=g(\widehat{\tau}(z))$ for every $z\in\Omega$;

(v) [the standing asymptotic conditions do not exclude it] for every integer $m\ge1$ repeat the four-unit block $m$ times: $N=4m$, $N_1=N_0=2m$, units indexed by $(b,j)$ with $b=1,\ldots,m$ and $j=1,\ldots,4$, $y_{(b,j)}=y_j$, $x_{(b,j)}=x_j$, $z^{(m)}_{A,(b,j)}=(z_A)_j$, $z^{(m)}_{B,(b,j)}=(z_B)_j$, $\widehat{\tau}(z)=\frac{1}{2m}\sum z\,y-\frac{1}{2m}\sum(1-z)\,y$, $L(z)=z_{(1,1)}y_{(1,1)}$ and $T(z)=\frac{1}{2m}\sum z\,x\,y-\frac{1}{2m}\sum(1-z)\,x\,y$. Then both assignments have exactly $2m$ treated units, $\widehat{\tau}=0$ at both with table $(m,m,m,m)$, $L(z^{(m)}_A)=1\ne0=L(z^{(m)}_B)$ and $T(z^{(m)}_A)=1/2\ne-1/2=T(z^{(m)}_B)$; $N_1/N=1/2$, and all outcomes and covariate values are in $\{0,1\}$, so the conditions of S2 hold.

As printed (p. 347): ‘As implied by Ding [(2017), equation (7)], we have that the variance of the FRT reference distribution, $$\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})=S_1^2/N_0+S_0^2/N_1+\tau^2/N+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right).$$ Suppose now that instead of using FRT, we were to use the Wald-type analogue considered by Freedman (2008), Gerber and Green (2012) and Samii and Aronow (2012). Ding (2017) notes the variance estimator associated with the Wald-type analogue in Section A.3.1, and refers to it as $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$, where $$\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})=S_1^2/N_0+S_0^2/N_1+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right).$$ [...] It follows that $$\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})=\tau^2/N+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right),$$ and the FRT is suboptimal given nonlocal alternatives. The reason for this is intuitive: in constructing the implied variance estimate, $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ fails to account for any location shift across potential outcomes (akin to the combined variance), whereas $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ does (akin to the pooled variance) (Reichardt and Gollob, 1999, pages 124–125).’ [The sentence omitted at ‘[...]’ is in target P053-T6.] As printed (p. 346, Introduction): ‘(i) Rao-type tests may be sub-optimal under nonlocal alternatives (Engle, 1984), and as Ding (2017) shows, the FRT is asymptotically equivalent to a Rao-type test assuming constant effects.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5, with $S_1^2$, $S_0^2$ and $\tau$ the quantities of the $N$th population:
(a) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_0}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_1}+\frac{\tau^2}{N}\right)\right]\to0$ in probability;
(b) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_0}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_1}\right)\right]\to0$ in probability;
(c) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\right]-\tau^2\to0$ in probability.
Consequently $N\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})\to\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\tau_\infty^2$ and $N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\to\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}$ in probability.

As printed (p. 347): ‘Freedman’s (2008) results straightforwardly imply that a Wald-type test using $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ is an asymptotically valid test of Fisher’s null, but that it may not be a valid test of the null that $\tau=0$ when treatment effects are heterogeneous (as is possible under Neyman’s null).’ As printed (p. 347): ‘Via standard linearization arguments, $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ will provide a better approximation to $V(\widehat{\tau})$ in large samples when effects are indeed nonlocal and constant.’ As printed (p. 347): ‘Under Pitman-type local alternatives of the form $\tau_N=o(1)$, we have $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})=o_p(N^{-1})$. The only asymptotic distinction between $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ and $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ emerges with nonlocal alternatives.’ As printed (p. 346, Introduction, item (i)): ‘Rather than the FRT, a Wald-type analogue to the FRT using the difference in means exists (considered by Freedman, 2008, Samii and Aronow, 2012, Gerber and Green, 2012, and Lin, 2013) is a valid test of Fisher’s null, and does not suffer from the potential pathology of Rao-type tests. Furthermore, this Wald-type test—equivalent to a pooled-variance two-sample $z$-test—is asymptotically equivalent to the FRT under local alternatives.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5:
(a) [local alternatives] if in addition $\tau_N\to0$, then $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\right]\to0$ in probability; if moreover $\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}>0$, then $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})/\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\to1$ in probability;
(b) [constant effects, of which Fisher's null is the case $\tau_N=0$] if $Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)=\tau_N$ for every $i$ and every $N$, then $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-V(\widehat{\tau})\right]\to0$ in probability and $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-V(\widehat{\tau})\right]-\tau_N^2\to0$ in probability;
(c) [asymptotic validity for Fisher's null] if $Y_i(1)=Y_i(0)$ for every $i$ and every $N$, and $\sigma_0^2>0$, then $\widehat{\tau}/\sqrt{\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})}$ converges in distribution to the standard normal law; hence, for $0<\alpha<1$, the test that rejects when $|\widehat{\tau}|/\sqrt{\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})}>z_{1-\alpha/2}$ (the standard normal quantile) has rejection probability tending to $\alpha$.
Part (c) rests on the finite-population central limit theorem, which the comment invokes on p. 347 (‘given the finite population central limit theorem’). The clause ‘it may not be a valid test of the null that $\tau=0$ when treatment effects are heterogeneous’ is part (c) of target P053-T8.

As printed (pp. 347–348): ‘Applying arguments from Neyman (1990), it is well known that $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})\le S_1^2/p+S_0^2/(1-p)$, where $p=\lim_{N\to\infty}N_1/N$. Ergo, given the finite population central limit theorem, Wald-type tests constructed using $\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ will tend to be conservative. Under constant effects, Neyman (1990) further shows that this holds with equality: $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=S_1^2/p+S_0^2/(1-p)$. Furthermore, if the maintained hypothesis of constant effects is true, then it must be the case that $S_1=S_0$ and, therefore, it would also be the case that $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=S_0^2/p+S_1^2/(1-p)$; in such a case, $\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ and $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ therefore asymptotically agree. If we are not in the world of constant effects, it is possible that $S_0\neq S_1$, and there exist limit distributions such that $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})>S_0^2/p+S_1^2/(1-p)$, implying that using $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ may yield anticonservative inferences and invalid tests of Neyman’s null.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5:
(a) [Neyman's bound] $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}-\sigma_\tau^2\le\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}$, the right side being the probability limit of $N\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$;
(b) [constant effects] if $Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)=\tau_N$ for every $i$ and every $N$, then $S_1=S_0$ and $S_\tau=0$ for every $N$, $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}=\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}$, and $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})\right]\to0$ in probability;
(c) [existence] there is a sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5 with $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})>\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}$, the right side being the probability limit of $N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$. Witness (the Sol reader's): for $m\ge1$ let $N=10m$, $N_1=2m$, $N_0=8m$, $Y_i(0)=1$ for $5m$ units and $Y_i(0)=-1$ for the other $5m$, and $Y_i(1)=2Y_i(0)$ for every $i$. Then $\tau=0$ (Neyman's null holds and the effects are heterogeneous), $S_0^2=S_\tau^2=\frac{N}{N-1}$, $S_1^2=\frac{4N}{N-1}$, and for every $m$: $V(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{81}{4(N-1)}$, $\frac{S_1^2}{N_0}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_1}=\frac{10}{N-1}$ and $\frac{S_1^2}{N_1}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_0}=\frac{85}{4(N-1)}$. So $p=\frac15$, $\sigma_1^2=4$, $\sigma_0^2=\sigma_\tau^2=1$, and $\lim NV(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{81}{4}$ lies above $10=\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}$ and below $\frac{85}{4}=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}$.
The words ‘will tend to be conservative’, ‘anticonservative inferences’ and ‘invalid tests of Neyman’s null’ are consequences drawn through the finite-population central limit theorem; the target is the variance statements.

As printed (pp. 346–347, Section 2): ‘Ding (2017) further reproduces Lin (2013) and Samii and Aronow’s (2012) result, showing that a Wald test from a linear model allowing for heteroskedasticity is equivalent to a Wald-type test using one of Neyman’s variance estimators, $$\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})=S_1^2/N_1+S_0^2/N_0+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right).$$’ As printed (p. 347, Section 3): ‘Ding [(2017), equation (7)], considers $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$; let us consider an analogue, replacing $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ with its Wald-type analogue $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$: $$\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})=\left(S_1^2/N_0+S_0^2/N_1\right)-\left(S_1^2/N_1+S_0^2/N_0\right)+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right).$$ A necessary condition for $\operatorname{plim}_{N\to\infty}N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\neq\operatorname{plim}_{N\to\infty}N\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ is $S_1\neq S_0$. [As noted above, this result also holds for $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ under any Pitman-type alternative such that $\tau_N=o(1)$.] We unpack this result.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5:
(a) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})-\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_1}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_0}\right)\right]\to0$ in probability;
(b) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})-\left(\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_0}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_1}\right)-\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_1}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_0}\right)\right)\right]\to0$ in probability;
(c) $N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\to\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}$ and $N\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})\to\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}$ in probability, and the difference of the two limits is $\frac{(\sigma_1^2-\sigma_0^2)(2p-1)}{p(1-p)}$; hence, if the two probability limits differ, then $\sigma_1\neq\sigma_0$ (and $p\neq\frac12$);
(d) if in addition $\tau_N\to0$, then (b) and (c) hold with $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ in place of $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$.
